182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (Rational agents maximize a given objective function, with their rationality defined by the structure of that function. The construction of such functions is inherently subjective, as different models may assign varying weights to factors like selfishness and selflessness.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (For example, Model A might define rational agents as entirely selfish, while Model B introduces a weighted balance of selfishness and altruism. While subjective, the development of these functions can be informed by empirical data. The Fehr-Schmidt model \(1999\) incorporates both selfishness and selflessness, drawing from experimental outcomes such as Roth et al. \(1991\) on the ultimatum game, highlighting the importance of both self-interest and altruism in defining rational behavior. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (We propose a rigorous AI driven methodology for constructing and refining objective functions of rational agents. By leveraging reinforcement learning \(RL\) and multi-agent simulation, we can model agent behavior under different assumptions of rationality. Specifically, we use deep RL to train simulated agents on synthetic datasets, where the agents iteratively adjust their utility-maximization strategies based on rewards defined by varying degrees of selfishness and altruism. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
/E (Our approach allows for the exploration of complex agent interactions and the emergence of nuanced behavioral patterns, advancing both the theoretical understanding and practical application of utility functions. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 156 0 R
