281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 727 0 R
/E (Beam search constructs utility specifications by expanding a library of symbolic expressions, base features and unary transformations of own payoff, others' mean payoff, and derived inequality terms across a discrete weight grid. At each depth, proposals are scored on training environments and the top candidates are retained for further expansion. Each candidate's final selection score is computed on held-out validation environments plus the complexity penalty.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 728 0 R
/E (Evolutionary search maintains a population of 8 symbolic utility specifications over several generations. The initial population is seeded with three theoretically motivated candidates \(pure selfishness, pure altruism, and the target social tradeoff form\) alongside randomly initialised specifications. The top third of each generation are retained as elites; the remainder are produced by mutation, which randomly adds, removes, or perturbs weight entries.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 729 0 R
/E (Exhaustive reference search evaluates compositional candidates plus three fixed baselines \(selfish, social tradeoff, inequity aversion\), totalling 31 candidates. This constitutes a finite restricted hypothesis class and serves as a reference benchmark; its winner is reported separately and is not eligible for primary selection.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
