/E (The exhaustive reference is the best utility function found by an exhaustive grid search over a restricted hypothesis class. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (It serves as a known benchmark: the best a simple, interpretable, one-term linear utility can achieve within a predefined grid, without search or optimisation. It is reported separately from the primary selection because it comes from a more restricted class than beam and evolutionary search, which can discover arbitrary symbolic transformations, multi-term combinations, and continuous weights. Comparing the primary winner against the exhaustive reference quantifies how much the richer search adds over straightforward enumeration of simple candidates.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
328 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (The exhaustive reference search, which enumerates single-term linear combinations, identified a scaled social feature as the best-fitting restricted-class utility, correctly recovering the social direction of the target though with an approximated weight. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
329 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (The primary selection winner, a two-term specification combining own-payoff and social components, achieved substantially lower mean test loss \(0.017 versus 0.058, p = 0.048\), demonstrating that the richer symbolic search adds meaningful predictive value beyond simple enumeration. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
330 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 170 0 R
/E (Notably, all three search methods independently converged on the social feature as the key predictive component, with the richer search additionally recovering the correct weight and the complementary contribution of the self-interest term.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
